---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { stats, jurisdictions, usingFixtures } from '../lib/source';
import { num } from '../lib/format';

const s = stats();
const js = jurisdictions();
const r = s.resolver ?? {};
const totalProbes = Object.values(r).reduce((a, b) => a + b.probes, 0);
const totalCorrect = Object.values(r).reduce((a, b) => a + b.correct, 0);
const withCode = js.filter((j) => j.code_sections > 0).length;
const withCases = js.filter((j) => j.cases > 0).length;
const dx = s.duluth_extraction;
---
<Base title="Status and freshness"
      description="What is loaded, what is verified, and what is knowingly missing."
      twin="/status.md">
  <h1>Status and freshness</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    What is loaded, what has actually been checked, and what is knowingly missing.
  </p>
  <p>
    An index that hides its staleness is worse than one that admits it, so the gaps below are
    published in the same place as the numbers.
  </p>

  <h2>Resolver accuracy</h2>
  <p>
    The jurisdiction resolver is scored against {num(s.probes)} probe points taken from the
    county's own zoning layers — the source that already carries the right answer.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Confidence</th><th>Probes</th><th>Correct</th><th>Rate</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {Object.entries(r).map(([k, v]) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{k}</td><td>{num(v.probes)}</td><td>{num(v.correct)}</td>
            <td>{v.probes ? `${((v.correct / v.probes) * 100).toFixed(2)}%` : '—'}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
        <tr>
          <td><strong>overall</strong></td><td>{num(totalProbes)}</td><td>{num(totalCorrect)}</td>
          <td>{totalProbes ? `${((totalCorrect / totalProbes) * 100).toFixed(2)}%` : '—'}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The first row is the one that matters: <strong>when the resolver reports high confidence it
    has never been wrong</strong>. Every error falls inside the low-confidence band, and every
    one of them was within 141 m of a jurisdiction line — annexation lag, since cities annex
    continuously and Census boundaries update yearly. Near a city limit, no boundary source is
    certain, and the resolver says so rather than guessing.
  </p>

  <h2>Coverage</h2>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>Jurisdictions</dt><dd>{num(js.length)} — all resolve; {withCases} have case records, {withCode} have code text</dd>
    <dt>Land-use cases</dt><dd>{num(s.total_cases)} ({num(s.county_cases)} county, {num(s.duluth_cases)} Duluth)</dd>
    <dt>Cases with a source link</dt><dd>{num(s.with_source_url)} of {num(s.total_cases)}</dd>
    <dt>Cases with a parcel PIN</dt><dd>{num(s.with_pins)}</dd>
    <dt>Code sections</dt><dd>{num(s.code_sections)}</dd>
    <dt>Code tables</dt><dd>{num(s.code_tables)}, of which {num(s.code_tables_verified)} verified</dd>
    <dt>Meeting documents</dt><dd>{num(s.meeting_docs)} ({num(s.meeting_pages)} pages)</dd>
    <dt>Build data source</dt><dd>{usingFixtures ? 'site/fixtures (SAMPLE)' : (s.generated_from ?? 'snapshot')}</dd>
    {s.generated_at && (<><dt>Snapshot taken</dt><dd>{s.generated_at.slice(0, 19).replace('T', ' ')} UTC</dd></>)}
  </dl>

  <h2>Known gaps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>{num(s.code_tables - s.code_tables_verified)} of {num(s.code_tables)} code tables are
      unverified.</strong> Only Table 2-B has been checked against the rendered source page, and
      even its merged PUD and CBD rows remain unreliable. Dimensional standards are the most
      queried content in the corpus and the easiest to get silently wrong.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>{num(s.merge_pending)} applicant name pairs await human review.</strong> They are
      queued rather than merged, so developer case counts are lower bounds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Duluth minutes are 57–69% scanned</strong> with no text layer. Where OCR is used the
      text is stored separately and marked as a reconstruction — it is never presented as a
      quotation of the record. The OCR pass has not been completed, so decisions and vote records
      are under-captured.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Duluth case fields are agenda-mined, not database-sourced.</strong> The city runs no
      case tracker, so these rows come out of agenda and minutes PDFs by pattern matching, and
      the field-level quality is poor. Measured across all {num(dx?.cases)} of them:
      <ul>
        <li>{num(dx?.location_without_street_number)} have a “location” containing no street
          number at all, {num(dx?.location_is_junk)} of which are plainly not addresses
          (<code>as presented.</code>, <code>&#123;J&#125;</code>)</li>
        <li>{num(dx?.request_is_boilerplate)} have a “request” that is just the word
          <code>ORDINANCE</code></li>
        <li>{num(dx?.applicant_overran)} have an applicant field that ran on into the applicant's
          mailing address, and {num(dx?.applicant_missing)} have none</li>
        <li>only {num(dx?.with_zoning)} carry a zoning district, and {num(dx?.with_outcome)} an outcome</li>
      </ul>
      Treat each of these as an <em>index entry pointing at a PDF</em>, not as a structured record.
      The linked document is the authority; the extracted fields are a finding aid.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>16 municipalities have boundaries but no corpus.</strong> They resolve correctly —
      the resolver's job is complete for the whole county — but there is no code or case data
      behind them yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Meeting body text is not published here</strong> (12.5 M characters). Only document
      metadata is indexed.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Legal posture</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Content</th><th>Posture</th><th>Basis</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Ordinance and plan text</td><td>Mirrored in full</td><td><em>Georgia v. Public.Resource.Org</em> (2020) — edicts of government</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Case records</td><td>Mirrored in full</td><td>Facts; Georgia Open Records Act</td></tr>
        <tr><td>County parcel and zoning geometry</td><td><strong>Never rehosted</strong></td><td>Gwinnett GIS licence forbids redistribution — queried live, linked out</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jurisdiction boundaries</td><td>Hosted</td><td>US Census TIGER — federal, public domain</td></tr>
        <tr><td>IBC/IRC base text</td><td><strong>Never indexed</strong></td><td>ICC copyright; Georgia's amendment packets are indexed instead</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</Base>
